        "text": "We need to work with the given spreadsheet. First, get summary of Population sheet to understand data.\nThen we need to calculate sample size for audit testing based on 90% confidence level and 10% tolerable error rate.\nSample size formula for proportion? Usually for attribute sampling: n = (Z^2 * p * (1-p)) / E^2, where Z is z-value for confidence level, p is expected error rate (often 0.5 for worst case), E is tolerable error rate.\nFor 90% confidence, Z = 1.645 (two-sided? Usually attribute sampling uses one-sided? But typical sample size for proportions uses Z for confidence level. We'll assume worst-case p=0.5.\nThus n = (1.645^2 * 0.5 * 0.5) / (0.1^2) = (2.706 * 0.25) / 0.01 = 0.6765 / 0.01 = 67.65 => 68.\nWe'll include workings.\n\nWe need to create a second tab titled 'Sample Size Calculation' and put workings there.\n\nThen perform variance analysis: Q2 in column H, Q3 in column G? Wait columns: According to header: A: No, B: Division, C: Sub-Division, D: Country, E: Legal Entity, F: KRIs, G: Q3 2024 KRI, H: Q2 2024 KRI.\nThus Q3 is G, Q2 is H. Variance = (G-H)/H. Put in column J. Flags in K? Actually they said flags in K, but later we need to mark sampled rows in column K by entering \"1\". So column K will be used for sample selection flag.\n\nWe need to compute variance for each row where H is not zero? If H zero, variance may be undefined; we can handle.\n\nThen select sample based on criteria. We'll need to evaluate each criterion and mark rows that satisfy at least one criterion, ensuring each criterion is satisfied by at least one selected sample.\n\nWe need to produce a sheet titled 'Sample' which is a copy of original 'Population' sheet with selected rows marked in column K (1). So we can copy the whole sheet to a new sheet named 'Sample', then set column K values for selected rows to 1.\n\nAlternatively we can create a new sheet and copy data, then mark.\n\nLet's start by getting sheet summary.\n",
